In article <4mg704$qbj@ictpsp10.ictp.trieste.it>,
Sergio Gelato <gelato@oort.ap.sissa.it> wrote:
>In article <4m9s2v$f6u@newton.cc.rl.ac.uk>,
>Rachel Kay Street <R.K.Street@rl.ac.uk> wrote:
>>2) Also any one tried to mix character*(*) array(length) and char *array[]
>>   (or char **array) when making calls from C into Fortran 77 functions ?
>
>I don't know about DEC's Fortran for OSF/1, but that particular mix is
>unlikely to work under other Unix'y implementations.

Actually, that mix will work under most flavors of Unix.  Among those
Unix's with which I am familiar, Fortran compilers under BSD 4.x,
SunOS, Solaris, OSF/1, AIX, and HP-UX, all support the BSD f77
convention for passing storing and passing [arrays of] character
strings.  (HP-UX and AIX have compile-time switches that let you select
between BSD's f77 string-passing convention and another convention.)
The BSD f77 rule is that a pointer to the start of the data is passed
in the argument's normal position in the calling list, and the length
of a character string is attached as an extra argument at the end of
the argument list.

>The equivalent of
>a Fortran CHARACTER array (CHARACTER*(N) array(length)) is usually something
>like char array[length][N], or in some cases char array[length*N].
>(On machines that are not byte-addressable, I would beware if N is
>not a multiple of the word/byte size ratio.)

I am pretty sure (but I don't have a Fortran manual handy to check on this)
that the rules for Fortran EQUIVALENCE statements between character strings
and character arrays guarantees that this layout holds for all Fortran
implementations.  (That is, an array of character strings is laid out in
memory with one character string immediately following the previous.)

-Will

