boc@ironbark.bendigo.latrobe.edu.au (Brian James O'Connor) writes:

>I'm getting a lot  (2-3 per hour) of the following error
>messages on my OSF(Digital Unix) 3.2b console

>NFS server: stale file handle fs(etc) file etc gen etc
> getattr, client address = blah, errno 22

That EINVAL is being returned from the file handle to vnode pointer
translation mechanism.  Unfortunately, more specific data isn't available
for UFS (I don't know about AdvFS), but the causes range from referencing
a deleted file (the standard cause of stale file handles) to the server
file system lacking resources to open the file (unlikely if the only
network interface is a single ethernet).

What is the client impact of this?

These server warning messages are meant to be useful in chasing down
twisty problems.  (A number of times network problems have been isolated
thanks to NFS's complaints.  Without those reports, people would have
had a much harder time figuring out why other stuff wasn't working.)
However, given that many customers get concerned about these, I'm considering
retiring all console messages that complain about stale file handles.
